From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Campos Ramos <Capitain_Jack@yahoo.com>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 00:33:54 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 3/8] feat(vk_native): declare a full side-by-side window to a
 stereo compositor (Wayland)

When the display processor's output is full side by side
(XRT_DP_SCANOUT_FLAG_FULL_SIDE_BY_SIDE), the Vulkan compositor presents a
target as wide as the views laid side by side (twice the window), hands the
DP that whole target as its canvas, and declares the app's native Wayland
surface once through kde_stereo_content_v1 (value 3: side by side, full width
per eye, left first). A stereo compositor such as Stereo KWin then shows each
view to its eye and turns the pair into what each output needs (a 3D
television's HDMI 3D mode, anaglyph on a 2D monitor, the left view alone on a
plain 2D output). From the protocol's version 2 a declared surface without a
viewport destination is one view wide, so the window keeps its size on
screen; the window, settings.preferred and the canvas the views are rendered
for keep the window's size, and the 1:1 gate measures one view.

The declaration is generated by wayland-scanner from the vendored XML (copied
from libstereo-declare, MIT-CMU) on a private event queue of the app's
connection, like the move-sync tag, and takes effect with the present that
carries the first buffer of the new width. It is re-read only when the DP or
the head's rendering mode changes, and withdrawn with the target back at the
window's size when the mode stops asking.

Nothing is presented as 3D that was not declared: on a compositor without
the global (or with version 1 only), on an X11 window, in a build without
wayland-scanner, or with DXR_STEREO_DECLARE=0, the target stays the window's
size and one FULL_SBS: line says why, so the window shows the two views side
by side, flat.

